MW–M31 Simulation Logbook — 2026-02-19
============================================================

Goal / Task:
------------
Upgrade toy Gaussian halo to a publication-grade NFW halo generator 
and implement isotropic Jeans velocity dispersion for stable MW/M31 
initial conditions.

Code Changes:
-------------
• Implemented sample_nfw_positions() using inverse-CDF sampling of 
  NFW cumulative mass.
• Implemented nfw_sigma() and sample_nfw_velocities() for Jeans-consistent 
  velocities.
• Added generate_mw_halo() and generate_m31_halo() for Milky Way and 
  Andromeda halos.
• Added generate_local_group() to combine MW + M31 with observational 
  separation (~780 kpc) and relative radial velocity (~−110 km/s).

Tests / Results:
----------------
• Generated 50k-particle MW halo; median radius ~80 kpc.
• Velocities sampled from Jeans dispersion; mean speed ~150 km/s.
• Halo visually stable for ~100 integration steps in Jupyter plots.
• Density profile roughly matches analytic NFW; minor truncation effects 
  near rmax.

Analysis / Notes:
-----------------
• Halo is significantly more stable than Gaussian toy model.
• Vectorized NumPy implementation is efficient and compatible with 
  Spyder/Jupyter workflow.
• Ready to integrate with existing Barnes–Hut leapfrog N-body code.
• Virial ratio check pending; optional velocity rescaling may be applied 
  before full MW–M31 merger simulation.

Next Steps:
-----------
• Validate virial ratio and adjust velocities if needed.
• Implement disk + bulge for both MW and M31.
• Run preliminary MW–M31 merger simulation and monitor energy conservation.
• Generate plots of density and velocity dispersion for methods appendix.
